Play! (https://github.com/jpd002/Play-, BSD licence, Copyright (c) 2006-2026 Jean-Philip Desjardins) runs the
PlayStation 2 on a thread of its own, a frame or two ahead of its libretro frontend. The tools apply this change of
Dreamports' own to its source before building it (tools/consoles.mjs, tools/libretro/build.mjs), so that each retro_run
is one frame of the console: the console waits at each V-blank, before it reads the buttons, until the frontend asks
for the next frame, the frontend serves the GS's calls until it has got there, and every block of sound is kept and
handed over with the frame it was mixed in, a block of 48 samples at a time (docs/CONSOLES.md, The PlayStation 2).

It also keeps each frame's draw list: a hash of everything the console gave the GS that decides its picture, in the order
given, every register write, every picture sent and what the screen shows of the framebuffer, which the frontend asks for
with dreamports_draw_list. That's what a 3D game is proven by (docs/STANDARD.md).

It keeps each frame's sound list as well, the same way: a hash of everything the console gave its sound chip, the SPU2,
in the order given, every register write, every block of sound data sent to or read from a core, and a mark for each
block of 48 samples mixed, so the list says which block each write came before. The frontend asks for it with
dreamports_sound_list, and DP_SOUND_DUMP names a file the whole list goes to, the sound data's bytes left out.

A picture the console reads back from the GS is in its memory before it goes on: the libretro front has no GS thread, so
Play! didn't wait for the read, and what a program read back changed from one run to the next (#1590).

Play!'s own SPU2 is not the console's in much: it mixes at 44,100 samples a second, steps between samples in a
straight line, has tables of its own for envelopes, a reverb in floats, and no noise, pitch modulation, master or
effect volume. So the kit's chip (kit/ps2/c/spu2.c, copied to Source/iop/dreamports/ as the tools build the emulator)
takes its place: the IOP's reads and writes of the chip's registers, its two DMA channels, its interrupt line and each
block of 48 samples go to it, and it is saved and loaded with the rest. The original and a Dreamport are then mixed by
the same code, which is checked against PCSX2's SPU2 (npm run test:ps2-spu2). DP_SOUND_DATA names a file for the bytes
moved to the chip by DMA, which that check gives PCSX2.

A picture the console reads back from the GS is in its memory before it goes on: the libretro front has no GS thread, so
Play! didn't wait for the read, and what a program read back changed from one run to the next (#1590).

A disc's kind is the disc's, not its file's: Play! takes any file of 2048-byte sectors for a DVD, so a CD kept as an ISO
was one too, where the console's drive says CD. It's judged as the kits judge it (kit/copy/disc.js): a DVD by its size or
by the UDF's marks after its volume (#1593).

When the environment's DP_DRAW_DUMP names a file, the whole list goes there too, from the first V-blank on, a picture's
bytes left out, for the tools to count what a game's lists hold (tools/lib/draw-lists.mjs).

And it tells the tools what the console does, when they ask before a game loads (dreamports_watch, Source/DreamportsWatch.h,
tools/libretro/ran-ps2.h): each block of instructions the EE, the IOP or a vector unit runs, as the block ends, with which
way its branch went and the words it was compiled from, on the EE and the IOP each instruction with what the registers
hold before it runs, every sector of the disc read, and every module the IOP loads, with where it put it and a hash of
the bytes it loaded, which says which module it is. A block that runs only says so, so the console does the same
with it or without; while the tools watch, the processors compile each block for itself rather than reuse the code of
another block of the same bytes, which would name that one.

It says where the console's memories are (dreamports_memory: the EE's, the IOP's and the SPU2's, each the console's size)
and its memory cards' files (dreamports_memory_card), with how many times the game has changed a card, for the tools to
compare with a Dreamport's (tools/libretro/held.h). And it gives the console a clock of its own, the same in every run:
midnight on 1 January 2001, Coordinated Universal Time, as it starts, going on as its V-blanks do, for the time the game
reads and for the times of a card's files, which Play! took from the computer's clock. How far the clock has got is
kept in a saved state, so a search that brings one back has the time it was saved at (tools/route.mjs).

And it says what the EE's registers hold as a block ends, and where it goes next (dreamports_registers), for the tools
to keep a function's start as play reaches it, with the memory it starts from, which the function check's calls then
start from (tools/libretro/starts.h, tools/ps2/twin-memories.mjs).

When DP_VU_DUMP names a file, the vector units' memories go there at each V-blank, vector unit 0's code and data and
then vector unit 1's, for the tools to compare with a Dreamport's (kit/ps2/c/vu.c, tools/test/ps2-ee-vu.mjs).

Its FPU and its vector units do their float sums by the console's rules, not a computer's: Source/ee/dreamports/floats.h
and floats.cpp are kit/ps2/c/floats.h and floats.c, word for word (tools/test/ps2-floats.test.mjs checks they are), and
Glue.cpp is what the recompiled code calls to reach them. With them the FPU keeps its flags, and the vector units' MAC
and status flags have underflow, overflow, invalid and division by zero with their sticky flags. The vector units'
timing follows the console's where Play! took a shortcut: a division's flags land with its Q, FSSET and FCSET are seen
four cycles on, a division and WAITQ take the cycles they wait and WAITP one fewer than P takes, every sum's MAC flags
are kept, the upper half's result is the one kept when both halves of a pair write a register, and everything a
program left on its way lands as it ends. SUBAq, which Play! left out, is there (kit/ps2/c/README.md says where each
rule comes from, and what the emulator still does its own way).

